13:48:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It's worth being careful about drawing broad conclusions from one visit, since that kind of observation can easily turn into an unfair stereotype about an entire people. That said, I can give you some accurate context. **Smoking rates in Iran:** Iran does have a notable smoking culture, though rates are actually comparable to or lower than many other countries, including parts of Europe. Estimates suggest maybe 10-15% of Iranian adults smoke cigarettes regularly, with rates significantly higher among men than women (smoking is more socially stigmatized for women in many contexts). **What your friend may have noticed:** - Cigarette smoking in cafes, on streets, and in some public areas (enforcement of indoor smoking bans varies) - Qalyan (hookah/waterpipe) culture is quite popular and visible, especially in tea houses and social gathering spots—this is often a social activity done in groups - Public smoking may simply be more visible in areas tourists frequent, like commercial districts or tourist-friendly cafes **Important nuances:** - "Persian" refers to an ethnicity, while Iran is home to many ethnic groups (Azeris, Kurds, Lurs, Baluchis, etc.), so it's more accurate to talk about "Iranians" if discussing the country broadly - Smoking habits vary widely by region, generation, and social class within Iran, just as they would in any country - Many countries have visible public smoking cultures that outside visitors notice—this isn't unique to Iran If you're curious about the topic, looking at WHO tobacco use data or Iranian public health studies would give you solid comparative statistics rather than relying on a single visitor's anecdotal impression.
