13:52:21 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Cigarette price is a real factor in tobacco economics generally—it's well-documented that lower prices/taxes correlate with higher smoking rates (this is why many countries use tobacco taxes as a public health tool). So the mechanism you're describing is legitimate in principle. **But it doesn't actually solve the puzzle you're implying:** If cheap cigarettes were sufficient to explain "everyone smokes," we'd expect smoking rates to be roughly uniform across the population, since price is the same for everyone nationally. But that's not what we see: - Smoking rates in Iran are dramatically lower among women (~2-3%) than men (~20-25%)—same price, huge gap. That gap is driven by social/religious norms, not economics. - Rates vary significantly by region, urban vs. rural, and generation—again, same price, different behavior. - Other countries with cheap cigarettes (parts of Eastern Europe, Indonesia, etc.) do have higher smoking rates, but plenty of countries with cheap cigarettes don't have "everyone smokes" as their reality either. **The actual academic view:** Price is one variable among several—social norms, religiosity, health campaigns, generational attitudes, gender expectations, and advertising/marketing regulation all interact with price to produce smoking behavior. "Cheap cigarettes" would predict elevated smoking rates relative to what they'd be if prices were higher (which is arguably true—Iran's rates might be even lower if cigarettes cost more), but it doesn't get you to "everyone smokes," because plenty of demographic groups within Iran, facing the same low prices, don't smoke at high rates at all. So: real factor, real effect on the margins, but not an explanation for a claim ("everyone smokes") that isn't itself accurate to begin with.
